WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.286
Here's our card payment again, written as JSON text: 116 bytes.

2
00:00:07.286 --> 00:00:09.033
Look at what fills them.

3
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:12.601
paymentId, merchant, amountMinor, currency, tags.

4
00:00:12.601 --> 00:00:17.041
The names of the fields, spelled out, in every single record.

5
00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:22.608
Even MessagePack, the binary form of JSON that keeps the names, needs 84 bytes.

6
00:00:22.608 --> 00:00:26.418
Drop the names, write just the five values, and it's 39.

7
00:00:26.418 --> 00:00:30.160
But then how does the reader know which value is which?

8
00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:34.288
Picture a café that takes catering orders on a paper form.

9
00:00:34.288 --> 00:00:39.494
Every box has a number: box 1 is the name, box 3 the amount, box 5 any notes.

10
00:00:39.494 --> 00:00:46.728
The cooks keep a blank copy on the wall, and read each order by box number, never by the printed questions.

11
00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:47.325
Map it.

12
00:00:47.325 --> 00:00:54.979
The blank form on the wall is a schema: a description of a record's fields and their types, kept apart from the data.

13
00:00:54.979 --> 00:00:56.484
Both sides keep a copy.

14
00:00:56.484 --> 00:00:58.970
And the box numbers are field numbers.

15
00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:02.111
They travel in the bytes, in place of the names.

16
00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:08.531
Protocol Buffers, or Protobuf, is the most common way to do this in Java and Kotlin programs.

17
00:01:08.531 --> 00:01:10.956
You write the form in a .proto file.

18
00:01:10.956 --> 00:01:14.189
Each line names a field, its type, and a number.

19
00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:17.220
And careful: equals 1 is not a default value.

20
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.567
It's the box number.

21
00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:23.619
It looks like a default, and that has fooled more of us than anyone admits.

22
00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:24.667
Now the bytes.

23
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:27.177
Each field becomes a key, then a value.

24
00:01:27.177 --> 00:01:32.902
The key packs two things into one number: the field number times eight, plus a wire type.

25
00:01:32.902 --> 00:01:37.020
The wire type tells the reader how to find where the value ends.

26
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.903
Type 0 is a varint, a whole number.

27
00:01:39.903 --> 00:01:45.154
Type 2 is a length, followed by that many bytes, for text and lists.

28
00:01:45.154 --> 00:01:50.096
Programmers write bytes in hex, base sixteen, two digits a byte.

29
00:01:50.096 --> 00:01:54.807
Field 1 is a varint, so its key is 1 times 8, plus 0: hex 08.

30
00:01:54.807 --> 00:01:56.043
Field 2 is text.

31
00:01:56.043 --> 00:01:57.201
What's its key?

32
00:01:57.201 --> 00:01:58.900
Pause and work it out.

33
00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:04.626
2 times 8 is 16, plus 2 is 18.

34
00:02:04.626 --> 00:02:06.162
In hex, that's 12.

35
00:02:06.162 --> 00:02:12.647
After it comes the length, 11, written 0b, then the 11 bytes of Fern Bakery.

36
00:02:12.833 --> 00:02:16.635
Whole numbers use a varint: as few bytes as the number needs.

37
00:02:16.635 --> 00:02:20.374
Each byte holds seven bits of the number, lowest bits first.

38
00:02:20.374 --> 00:02:24.736
Its eighth bit, the top one, is a little flag that says: more to come.

39
00:02:24.933 --> 00:02:27.010
Take the amount, 4000.

40
00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:34.468
Its lowest seven bits are worth 32, and more is coming, so the flag is set: a0.

41
00:02:34.468 --> 00:02:38.527
What's left is 31, with the flag clear: 1f.

42
00:02:38.527 --> 00:02:41.171
So 4000 is two bytes, a0 1f.

43
00:02:41.171 --> 00:02:45.041
Check it: 32, plus 31 times 128, is 4000.

44
00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:47.181
Now the whole payment.

45
00:02:47.181 --> 00:02:49.746
Field 1, the payment number.

46
00:02:49.746 --> 00:02:51.669
Field 2, Fern Bakery.

47
00:02:51.669 --> 00:02:52.951
Field 3, 4000.

48
00:02:52.951 --> 00:02:54.142
Field 4, EUR.

49
00:02:54.142 --> 00:02:57.072
And field 5 twice, once per tag.

50
00:02:57.072 --> 00:02:59.911
42 bytes, against 116 for JSON.

51
00:02:59.911 --> 00:03:04.399
Numbers, wire types and values, and not one name.

52
00:03:04.567 --> 00:03:07.194
So should every number be a varint?

53
00:03:07.194 --> 00:03:08.846
Let's be fair to them.

54
00:03:08.846 --> 00:03:11.398
Anything below 128 takes one byte.

55
00:03:11.398 --> 00:03:15.152
But the payment number is huge and random-looking.

56
00:03:15.152 --> 00:03:17.329
As a varint it takes 9 bytes.

57
00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:21.759
Declared as fixed64, a type that always uses 8, it takes 8.

58
00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:24.274
Negative numbers are worse.

59
00:03:24.274 --> 00:03:31.707
A computer stores minus 1 with all 64 bits set, so a plain int64 needs 10 bytes for it.

60
00:03:31.707 --> 00:03:40.337
The type sint64 first applies ZigZag: 0 becomes 0, minus 1 becomes 1, 1 becomes 2, minus 2 becomes 3.

61
00:03:40.337 --> 00:03:42.729
Then minus 1 takes one byte.

62
00:03:42.867 --> 00:03:45.373
So the type you declare decides the bytes.

63
00:03:45.373 --> 00:03:47.403
Pick it for the values you expect.

64
00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:52.626
So: put a number on each field, and let the bytes carry numbers, not names.

65
00:03:52.626 --> 00:03:56.532
Both sides keep the form, and every value says how long it is.

66
00:03:56.733 --> 00:03:57.979
One quick check.

67
00:03:57.979 --> 00:04:00.549
Field 3, the amount, is a varint.

68
00:04:00.549 --> 00:04:02.106
What's its key byte?

69
00:04:02.106 --> 00:04:03.352
Pause and think.

70
00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.313
3 times 8, plus 0, is 24.

71
00:04:09.313 --> 00:04:10.507
In hex, 18.

72
00:04:10.507 --> 00:04:13.980
That's the 18 in front of a0 1f.

73
00:04:14.167 --> 00:04:18.545
And every value says how long it is: that matters more than it looks.

74
00:04:18.545 --> 00:04:24.574
A reader that didn't know field 5 could still read its length and skip exactly that many bytes.

75
00:04:24.574 --> 00:04:29.269
Next, the café adds box 6, and the night cook still has last month's form.
